      "html": "<section class=\"ch wrap\"><div class=\"g4\"><div><div class=\"num\">03</div><div class=\"lab\">Problem</div></div><div class=\"c3\"><h2 class=\"h2\">The ask was speed. The problem was evidence, and who the screen should trust.</h2><p class=\"lead\">Refund decisions often hinge on thin evidence. An order-centred agent panel brings policy checks, evidence and each party's history into one review, to cut investigation effort and protect genuine claims.</p><div class=\"mod\"><div class=\"ref\"><div class=\"a\"><div class=\"k\">The brief</div><div class=\"t\">Cut the time it takes an agent to reach a refund decision.</div><ul><li>One view of chat, order, evidence, history and policy</li><li>Accurate</li><li>Fair to three parties</li></ul></div><div class=\"b\"><div class=\"k\">The real problem</div><div class=\"t\">Decisions hinge on thin evidence, and a faster screen that recommends would be trusted too much.</div><ul><li>Up to 25% of refunds in a Swiggy-group study went wrong, mostly on weak photos <span class=\"src\">· AWS case study, Supr Daily</span></li><li>912 of 10,590 helpline complaints against Swiggy in 2024 were about unprocessed refunds <span class=\"src\">· reported, May 2025</span></li><li>Training cannot stop people over-trusting automated advice <span class=\"src\">· Parasuraman and Manzey, 2010</span></li></ul></div></div></div></div></div></section>"
